In article <1992Dec1.234250.13131@news.columbia.edu> dan@cubmol.bio.columbia.edu (Daniel Zabetakis) writes:
>   No, I think you miss the point. The school was providing a service, and
>a valued one at that. They removed access to it apparently because (solely)
>it was being investigated as having contained an illegal object at one time.
>   There was no mention of resource limitations. 
>
>   Under these conditions, the action taken by the administration was a clear
>violation of academic freedom (assuming of course that you view computers
>as applicable to AF).

I have spoken with some of those responsible for the restriction of the 
alt.*.erotica newsgroups here at Cornell. I don't believe that the _only_ 
reason these groups were cut off was because of legal investigation. I know 
that if I was responsible for the newsstand here, and was aware that it was 
being directly abused (illegally so), that the easiest and most effective way 
to deal with the problem is to simply cut off the newsgroup. Cornell is in 
NO WAY obligated to provide those newsgroups to the Cornell community, in the 
same way that it's not obligated to carry 'Playboy' in the circulars at the 
undergrad library.

Furthermore, the alt.*.erotica newsgroups aren't an educational resource.
I don't care how broadly you define Academic Freedom, this doesn't
OBLIGE Cornell to carry those groups regardless of their content or abuse
on the net. Simply because Cornell itself doesn't propagate the groups
across its subnetworks doesn't mean that those who wish to have access to
the groups (via other USENET feeds, public UNIX systems, and so forth) are
restricted from doing so. 

It seems that you liken the cutting off of the erotica groups here to the
banning of an 'obscene' book in a campus library. I think the lamp of
academia can shine on without the erotica newsgroups being made available
here. You're looking at it the wrong way: Cornell has ZERO obligation to
provide these groups. Do you scream and yell because de.fahrvegnugen isn't
available at your site? I doubt it. 

Seriously, however, I think the point is not that this is restricting access
to information. You have to look at it from the news and system admin's
point of view, which is that their systems are involved in something which
is causing quite an upset, at least with the legal authorities. I wouldn't
want to have to constantly police the actions of my users to ensure that
they wern't breaking the law. It's simply too much of a pain to deal with 
those groups in this situation, and the primary concern of a system 
administrator is the system, not the political garbage that surrounds the
information propagated on it. 

Most system admins like to "stay out of trouble." I can sympathize
with that. It's not like Cornell sent out rmgroup messages for those
groups; they're still available. Just not directly from Cornell machines.

mdw

